# Test Scenario Rules

- Spacing
  - 2 lines between scenarios
- Use `Scenario:` for specific flows
- Use `Scenario Outline:` with an `Examples:` table for variations

## Test Scenario Title

- Instructions
  - Write distinct Scenario Titles for the test feature
  - One clear action or condition per scenario
  - Use natural language to describe what is being verified
  - Tie the scenario to the "So that" part of the user story
  - Cover both positive and negative cases
- Avoid 
  - Using Examples parameter in the title (something like <parameter_name>)
  - Indicating a specific value to be set in the settings
- Examples
  - Good Examples
    - Login with valid credentials
    - Prevent login with incorrect password
    - Change the price label
  - Bad Examples
    - Set the price label to "Cost"
    - Show <price_value> to the customer

## Test Scenario Description

- Instructions
  - Write a short description for the test scenario
  - Explain what the scenario is testing
  - Explain what the difference from the other scenarios
- Examples
  - Good Examples;
    - Ensure a registered user can successfully log in using correct email and password to access their dashboard
    - Verify that users are blocked from logging in if the password is incorrect, and a helpful error is displayed

## Test Scenario Steps

- Instructions
  - Write Given/When/Then steps using Gherkin syntax
    - Given: Preconditions or setup
      - Use passive voice
    - When: User actions
      - Use "the user" instead of "I"
    - Then: Outcome or assertion
      - Use "should" for the assertion
  - Use simple present tense
  - Lowercase the first letter after each keyword
  - End steps without punctuation (unless followed by table or doc string)
  - Use declarative language focused on what happens (not how)
  - Follow the strict order: Given -> When -> Then
  - Write modular, reusable steps
  - Make sure to reuse the steps in `Existing scenario steps` reference
- Avoid
  - Mixing Gherking keyword order
- Examples
  - Good Examples:
    - Example 1
      ```
      Given the user has an account
      When the user submits correct login credentials
      Then the user should be redirected to the dashboard
      ```
    - Example 2
      ```
      Given the "WP Admin > Settings > General > Site Title" is set to "Test Site"
      When the "admin" user logs in
      And the user navigates to the "Home" page
      Then the page title should be "Test Site"
      ```
    - Example 3 (doc string and datatable)
      ```
      Then the page should show the following message:
        """
        Success! Your changes have been saved.
        """
      And the product has the following info:
        | Info  | Value   |
        | Price | $123.00 |
        | Size  | Big     |
      ```
  - Bad Examples
    - Example 1
      ```
      Given the user has an account
      When the user submits correct login credentials
      Then the user should be redirected to the dashboard
      When the user navigates to the "Cart" page
      Then the user should be redirected to the "Cart" page
      ```
    - Example 2
      ```
      Given the customer is opening the "Cart" page
      And the Cart page title is "Cart"
      When I navigate to the "Cart" page
      Then the page title is "Cart"
      ```

