# Test Feature And Test Scenario Tags

- Instructions
  - Tag features and scenarios for traceability
  - Tags go directly above Feature or Scenario blocks
  - Use `@` prefix for all tags
  - Remove scenario-level tags if all scenarios use the same set (move to the top of test feature)
- Tag categories
  - @current-sprint: Part of current sprint
  - @needs:automation: Not yet automated
  - @needs:automation-fixes: Automation broken
  - @[project-code]-issue-[id]: Related GitHub issue
  - @smoke-test: Critical regression checks
  - @feature:[name]: Plugin feature
  - @user:[role]: User role involved
  - @area:[location]: Page or component
  - @setting:[path]:[to]:[setting]: Specific setting

---

# Example

```
@smoke-test
@feature:customer-login @user:customer @area:login-page
Feature: Allow customer to log in
  As a customer
  I want to log in to the system
  So that I can access the registered-customer only features


  Background: Needed customer account is created
    Given the "customer" account is created


  Scenario: Login with valid credentials
    Ensure a registered user can successfully log in using correct email and password to access their dashboard
        
    When the user visits the "login" page
    And the user fills the login form with "customer_account" as the username and "account_password" as the password
    Then the user should be logged in
    And the page should show notification of:
      """
      Success logged in.
      Please visit documentation to learn more about the features.
      """
    And the page should show login history table:
      | loginDate  | loginIP         |
      | 02-12-2222 | 111.111.111.111 |
      | 01-12-2222 | 222.222.222.222 |
    
  
  Scenario Outline: Prevent login with incorrect password
    Verify that users are blocked from logging in if the password is incorrect, and a helpful error is displayed

    When the user visits the "login" page
    And the user fills the login form with "<username>" as the username and "<password>" as the password
    Then the user shouldn't be logged in
    And the login page should show "<error_message>" error message
    
    Examples:
    | username         | password         | error_message                                  |
    | wrong_username   | account_password | Unable to log in. Wrong username              |
    | customer_account | wrong_password   | Unable to log in. Wrong password              |
    | wrong_username   | wrong_password   | Unable to log in. Wrong username and password |
```